Nothing much in NetHack happens "all of a sudden".

NetHack is a one-turn-at-a-time game like chess,
not a twitch reflex game like Doom.

You have all the time you need to look at the
results after each move.

The fastest way to die in NetHack is typing fast.

Play the game one move at a time.

Then stop, look around, to see if any monster is
approaching.

If so, do something, like getting yourself into a
corridor, to avoid being attacked in melee by more
than two monsters at once.

Look at your stats line, look at your message
display, so as not to let any changes sneak by you.

[pets]

Pets not only fight on your side, but perhaps more
important, are great damage sponges. If you and your
pet are fighting a monster, it will attack your pet
on average half the time, giving you twice as long
to kill the monster before _your_ hit points are
gone, and, if your pet gets in any successful
attacks, that just helps you kill the monster even
faster.

The downsides to pets are several.

1) They'll grab appropriate food and corpses if they
can get to them before you do, putting you at risk
of starving (but learn about food prayers, Sokoban,
bigger more nutritious monsters further down in the
dungeon).

2) They're a pain to herd where you want them to go.

3) Herbivors tend to starve fairly quickly.

4) Pets attack monsters while ignoring in their
decision whether or not to attack all but the
monster's XL, so that monsters equipped with
weapons, armor, or special attacks like spells or
multiple hits per turn often find your pet an easy
kill [the solution is to keep your pet under your
control, or join the attack alongside your pet, but
see "2)"].

5) Pets fall through trap doors or step on level
teleporter traps far too often to make you cheerful.

There are too many additional upsides about pets to
list, but the obvious ones are:

1) They often find traps before you do, so you can
avoid those traps.

2) They are fast, sometimes getting multiple attacks
per turn on monsters.

3) They gain levels fast, though in the case of your
starting pet, at most to XL:9 HP:72. This makes them
better fighters and better damage sponges, often
better than you are in the early game.

4) They'll steal shop goods from shops for you.

5) They'll steal gold from the floor of shops for
you, making "credit cloning" possible, which in turn
makes buying protection from priests easier.

6) They can help you avoid wearing, putting on, or
wielding cursed items, by stepping happily on
uncursed stuff and only "reluctantly" on cursed
stuff.

7) They'll kill peaceful monsters for you, so you
can get those monster's goodies or corpses without
penalties.

8) About half the time, there is a turn between them
killing a monster, and them eating that monster,
letting you grab the corpse first for your own meal.
Do remember not to eat your own species-group
though, one of the kinds of monsters likely to be
peaceful toward you.

9) They can carry ridiculously large items for you,
particularly warhorses can, though not very
controllably, letting you avoid being "stressed".

10) They can be polymorphed into better pets,
sometimes.

11) More of them, or replacements, are easily
acquired from about DLVL 4 and down, by throwing
them appropriate treats, tripe or human rations for
carnivores, fruit or vegetables for herbivores.

